{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X5p9UcSaiFBpKdcnNEn652","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd002-sys\n     2→title: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n     6→  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n     7→  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n     8→  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n     9→  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n    10→  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n    11→  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n    12→  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n    13→\n    14→  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n    15→  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n    16→  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n    20→  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n    21→  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n    22→\n    23→requirements:\n    24→  R1:\n    25→    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    26→    items:\n    27→      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n    28→      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n    29→      - R1.3: |\n    30→          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n    31→          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n    32→          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n    33→          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n    34→          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n    35→          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n    36→          output when stdout is redirected.\n    37→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n    38→      - R1.5: |\n    39→          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n    40→          process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n    41→          Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n    42→          size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n    43→          use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n    44→          GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n    45→      - R1.6: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n    46→      - R1.7: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n    47→\n    48→  R2:\n    49→    title: Extended file metadata\n    50→    items:\n    51→      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n    52→      - R2.2: |\n    53→          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n    54→\n    55→            type FileInfo struct {\n    56→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n    57→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    58→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    59→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    60→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    61→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    62→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    63→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    64→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    65→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    66→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n    67→                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n    68→            }\n    69→      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n    70→      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n    71→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n    72→      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n    73→\n    74→  R3:\n    75→    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    76→    items:\n    77→      - R3.1: |\n    78→          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n    79→      - R3.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n    80→      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n    81→\n    82→non_goals:\n    83→  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n    84→  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n    85→  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n    86→  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n    87→\n    88→acceptance_criteria:\n    89→  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n    90→  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n    91→  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n    92→  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n    93→  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n    94→  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    95→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a95a5d2-6a7e-4f46-be9a-8dc65ffccd7e","uuid":"67bfe6e9-16fe-4355-993f-e1b342cb8ac6","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/295/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml","content":"id: prd002-sys\ntitle: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n\n  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n      - R1.3: |\n          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n          output when stdout is redirected.\n      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n      - R1.5: |\n          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n          process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n          Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n          size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n          use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n          GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n      - R1.6: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n      - R1.7: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: Extended file metadata\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n      - R2.2: |\n          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n            }\n      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n\n  R3:\n    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n      - R3.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HzZyZKJbihs1B8xdGsPXG9","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel00.0-uc003-sys\n     2→title: \"Query terminal width, stat files, and handle signals via pkg/sys\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator verifies that pkg/sys provides correct terminal-width queries, extended\n     6→  file metadata, and signal handling on both Darwin and Linux. The test exercises each\n     7→  capability in isolation: TerminalWidth returns a positive column count on a TTY,\n     8→  IsTerminal distinguishes terminals from pipes, Stat and Lstat return correct extended\n     9→  metadata, InstallSIGPIPEHandler suppresses broken-pipe errors, and OnTerminalResize\n    10→  invokes callbacks on SIGWINCH.\n    11→\n    12→actor: Human Operator\n    13→\n    14→trigger: |\n    15→  The operator has completed the PRD revision (prd002-sys.yaml) and wants to verify that\n    16→  pkg/sys satisfies its requirements via unit tests.\n    17→\n    18→flow:\n    19→  - id: F1\n    20→    step: |\n    21→      The operator runs go test ./pkg/sys/... which executes the unit tests for pkg/sys.\n    22→      Unlike cmd/ packages, pkg/sys does not use differential testing because there is no\n    23→      reference binary — it is a library.\n    24→  - id: F2\n    25→    step: |\n    26→      The TerminalWidth test calls TerminalWidth() and verifies it returns a positive\n    27→      integer when stdout is a terminal, or an error when stdout is a pipe.\n    28→  - id: F3\n    29→    step: |\n    30→      The IsTerminal test calls IsTerminal with a pipe fd and verifies it returns false.\n    31→      It calls IsTerminal with a TTY fd (if available in the test environment) and verifies\n    32→      it returns true.\n    33→  - id: F4\n    34→    step: |\n    35→      The Stat test calls Stat on a known file and verifies Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino,\n    36→      and Blocks match the output of stat(1) on the same file.\n    37→  - id: F5\n    38→    step: |\n    39→      The Lstat test calls Lstat on a symbolic link and verifies that the returned FileInfo\n    40→      has type bits showing symlink, not the target's mode.\n    41→  - id: F6\n    42→    step: |\n    43→      The fillFromStat test verifies that the Darwin/Linux abstraction correctly populates\n    44→      FileInfo.ModTime from st_mtimespec (Darwin) or st_mtim (Linux).\n    45→  - id: F7\n    46→    step: |\n    47→      The InstallSIGPIPEHandler test verifies that writing to a broken pipe after installing\n    48→      the handler causes the process to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\n    49→\n    50→touchpoints:\n    51→  - T1: \"pkg/sys — TerminalWidth, IsTerminal, InstallSIGPIPEHandler (prd002-sys R1)\"\n    52→  - T2: \"pkg/sys — Stat, Lstat, FileInfo, fillFromStat (prd002-sys R2)\"\n    53→  - T3: \"pkg/sys — OnTerminalResize (prd002-sys R3)\"\n    54→\n    55→success_criteria:\n    56→  - id: S1\n    57→    criterion: TerminalWidth returns a positive integer on a TTY or an error on a pipe.\n    58→  - id: S2\n    59→    criterion: IsTerminal(pipeFd) returns false.\n    60→  - id: S3\n    61→    criterion: Stat on a known file returns correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, Blocks.\n    62→  - id: S4\n    63→    criterion: Lstat on a symlink returns type bits showing symlink.\n    64→  - id: S5\n    65→    criterion: FileInfo.ModTime is populated from the platform-correct field.\n    66→  - id: S6\n    67→    criterion: InstallSIGPIPEHandler causes exit 0 on broken pipe.\n    68→  - id: S7\n    69→    criterion: go test ./pkg/sys/... passes on darwin/arm64 with no failures.\n    70→\n    71→out_of_scope:\n    72→  - SIGWINCH testing requires a real terminal resize event; it is tested manually, not in the harness.\n    73→  - Process-group management (setpgid, killpg) is deferred to the xargs PRD.\n    74→  - SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 handling is deferred to the find PRD.\n    75→\n    76→test_suite: test-rel00.0\n    77→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a95a5d2-6a7e-4f46-be9a-8dc65ffccd7e","uuid":"1827d7e7-3f84-4c1f-8f92-97d378d2733b","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/295/docs/specs/use-cases/rel00.0-uc003-sys.yaml","content":"id: rel00.0-uc003-sys\ntitle: \"Query terminal width, stat files, and handle signals via pkg/sys\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator verifies that pkg/sys provides correct terminal-width queries, extended\n  file metadata, and signal handling on both Darwin and Linux. The test exercises each\n  capability in isolation: TerminalWidth returns a positive column count on a TTY,\n  IsTerminal distinguishes terminals from pipes, Stat and Lstat return correct extended\n  metadata, InstallSIGPIPEHandler suppresses broken-pipe errors, and OnTerminalResize\n  invokes callbacks on SIGWINCH.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed the PRD revision (prd002-sys.yaml) and wants to verify that\n  pkg/sys satisfies its requirements via unit tests.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs go test ./pkg/sys/... which executes the unit tests for pkg/sys.\n      Unlike cmd/ packages, pkg/sys does not use differential testing because there is no\n      reference binary — it is a library.\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      The TerminalWidth test calls TerminalWidth() and verifies it returns a positive\n      integer when stdout is a terminal, or an error when stdout is a pipe.\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      The IsTerminal test calls IsTerminal with a pipe fd and verifies it returns false.\n      It calls IsTerminal with a TTY fd (if available in the test environment) and verifies\n      it returns true.\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      The Stat test calls Stat on a known file and verifies Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino,\n      and Blocks match the output of stat(1) on the same file.\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The Lstat test calls Lstat on a symbolic link and verifies that the returned FileInfo\n      has type bits showing symlink, not the target's mode.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      The fillFromStat test verifies that the Darwin/Linux abstraction correctly populates\n      FileInfo.ModTime from st_mtimespec (Darwin) or st_mtim (Linux).\n  - id: F7\n    step: |\n      The InstallSIGPIPEHandler test verifies that writing to a broken pipe after installing\n      the handler causes the process to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"pkg/sys — TerminalWidth, IsTerminal, InstallSIGPIPEHandler (prd002-sys R1)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/sys — Stat, Lstat, FileInfo, fillFromStat (prd002-sys R2)\"\n  - T3: \"pkg/sys — OnTerminalResize (prd002-sys R3)\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: TerminalWidth returns a positive integer on a TTY or an error on a pipe.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: IsTerminal(pipeFd) returns false.\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: Stat on a known file returns correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, Blocks.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: Lstat on a symlink returns type bits showing symlink.\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: FileInfo.ModTime is populated from the platform-correct field.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: InstallSIGPIPEHandler causes exit 0 on broken pipe.\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: go test ./pkg/sys/... passes on darwin/arm64 with no failures.\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - SIGWINCH testing requires a real terminal resize event; it is tested manually, not in the harness.\n  - Process-group management (setpgid, killpg) is deferred to the xargs PRD.\n  - SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 handling is deferred to the find PRD.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel00.0\n","numLines":77,"startLine":1,"totalLines":77}}}
