{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011X5zMWNArqLax7fADgXJF4","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Golangci-lint v2 configuration for go-unix-utils.\n     2→# Covers cmd/, pkg/, and magefiles/. Implements prd011-magefiles R3.3.\n     3→\n     4→version: \"2\"\n     5→\n     6→run:\n     7→  go: \"1.25\"\n     8→\n     9→linters:\n    10→  default: none\n    11→  enable:\n    12→    - errcheck          # check return values of error-returning functions\n    13→    - govet             # vet-like checks (printf format strings, composite literals, etc.)\n    14→    - staticcheck       # comprehensive static analysis (SA* rules; absorbs gosimple in v2)\n    15→    - ineffassign       # detect ineffectual assignments\n    16→    - unused            # detect unused exported and unexported identifiers\n    17→    - revive            # idiomatic Go style (godoc on exported symbols, naming, etc.)\n    18→    - gochecknoglobals  # flag package-level variables; suppressed per-file with //nolint\n    19→    - misspell          # detect common spelling mistakes in comments and strings\n    20→\n    21→  settings:\n    22→    errcheck:\n    23→      # fmt print functions write to stdout/stderr; errors are non-actionable at the call site.\n    24→      exclude-functions:\n    25→        - fmt.Print\n    26→        - fmt.Println\n    27→        - fmt.Printf\n    28→        - fmt.Fprint\n    29→        - fmt.Fprintln\n    30→        - fmt.Fprintf\n    31→    revive:\n    32→      rules:\n    33→        - name: exported\n    34→          severity: warning\n    35→          disabled: false\n    36→        - name: var-naming\n    37→          severity: warning\n    38→          disabled: false\n    39→\n    40→  exclusions:\n    41→    rules:\n    42→      # Test files commonly use package-level variables for shared fixtures (e.g.,\n    43→      # table-driven test inputs, reusable test options). These are intentional.\n    44→      - path: \"_test\\\\.go\"\n    45→        linters:\n    46→          - gochecknoglobals\n    47→      # magefiles/ is build tooling provided by cobbler-scaffold. The package-level\n    48→      # baseCfg variable is required by the orchestrator init contract (R6.2). Helper\n    49→      # functions such as logf are scaffold-provided and may have no callers in the\n    50→      # current magefiles set.\n    51→      - path: magefiles/\n    52→        linters:\n    53→          - gochecknoglobals\n    54→          - unused\n    55→      # staticcheck SA4017 (\"X is never called\") for magefiles/ scaffold helpers.\n    56→      - path: magefiles/\n    57→        text: \"is never called\"\n    58→        linters:\n    59→          - staticcheck\n    60→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a95a5d2-6a7e-4f46-be9a-8dc65ffccd7e","uuid":"bb9c90bd-3616-438f-becf-29b9f3d1a799","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/295/.golangci.yml","content":"# Golangci-lint v2 configuration for go-unix-utils.\n# Covers cmd/, pkg/, and magefiles/. Implements prd011-magefiles R3.3.\n\nversion: \"2\"\n\nrun:\n  go: \"1.25\"\n\nlinters:\n  default: none\n  enable:\n    - errcheck          # check return values of error-returning functions\n    - govet             # vet-like checks (printf format strings, composite literals, etc.)\n    - staticcheck       # comprehensive static analysis (SA* rules; absorbs gosimple in v2)\n    - ineffassign       # detect ineffectual assignments\n    - unused            # detect unused exported and unexported identifiers\n    - revive            # idiomatic Go style (godoc on exported symbols, naming, etc.)\n    - gochecknoglobals  # flag package-level variables; suppressed per-file with //nolint\n    - misspell          # detect common spelling mistakes in comments and strings\n\n  settings:\n    errcheck:\n      # fmt print functions write to stdout/stderr; errors are non-actionable at the call site.\n      exclude-functions:\n        - fmt.Print\n        - fmt.Println\n        - fmt.Printf\n        - fmt.Fprint\n        - fmt.Fprintln\n        - fmt.Fprintf\n    revive:\n      rules:\n        - name: exported\n          severity: warning\n          disabled: false\n        - name: var-naming\n          severity: warning\n          disabled: false\n\n  exclusions:\n    rules:\n      # Test files commonly use package-level variables for shared fixtures (e.g.,\n      # table-driven test inputs, reusable test options). These are intentional.\n      - path: \"_test\\\\.go\"\n        linters:\n          - gochecknoglobals\n      # magefiles/ is build tooling provided by cobbler-scaffold. The package-level\n      # baseCfg variable is required by the orchestrator init contract (R6.2). Helper\n      # functions such as logf are scaffold-provided and may have no callers in the\n      # current magefiles set.\n      - path: magefiles/\n        linters:\n          - gochecknoglobals\n          - unused\n      # staticcheck SA4017 (\"X is never called\") for magefiles/ scaffold helpers.\n      - path: magefiles/\n        text: \"is never called\"\n        linters:\n          - staticcheck\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":60}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_017pq8MAgDpW1ptdz6GHyNC4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. The linters enabled are:\n- errcheck: check return values\n- govet: vet checks\n- staticcheck: static analysis\n- ineffassign: ineffectual assignments\n- unused: unused identifiers\n- revive: godoc on exported symbols, naming, etc.\n- gochecknoglobals: flag package-level variables (suppressed in test files)\n- misspell: spelling mistakesThe `errcheck` excludes fmt.Print* functions.\n\nNow I need to be careful about:\n1. Every exported symbol needs godoc comments (revive: exported)\n2. Package-level variables need `//nolint:gochecknoglobals` in non-test files\n\n\n3. All errors must be checked (errcheck)\n\nThe `fmt.Fprint` functions are excluded from errcheck, which works well since I'm using those in signal handler goroutines. Now I'm thinking about the build tags for sys.go — the `golang.org/x/sys/unix` package compiles on all Unix systems without needing build tags, but I'll need them for the platform-specific `fillFromStat` implementations in sys_darwin.go and sys_linux.go.\n\nFor Go 1.17+, `//go:build` is the preferred format over `// +build`, and since this module targets Go 1.25.0, that's what I should use. Now I need to handle the `Blksize` field carefully — it's int64 in the FileInfo struct, but the underlying system calls return different types on different platforms, so I'll need to cast appropriately when populating it.\n\nLooking at the task notes about Linux specifically, `st_blocks` is already counted in 512-byte blocks, so I shouldn't multiply it again when filling the Blocks field. The `st_blksize` value represents the preferred I/O block size for efficient operations. /O block size (typically 4096 bytes). `st_blocks` is the number of 512-byte blocks allocated.\n\nI think the task note \"*512\" is a typo or mistake. The field should just be `st.Blocks` directly since it's already in 512-byte units on Linux.\n\nActually wait. Looking at the implementation in `cmd/cat/main.go` and other files - they don't use pkg/sys at all yet. The pkg/sys is being created now.\n\nI'll keep `fi.Blocks = st.Blocks` for Linux (no multiplication needed) since st_blocks is already in 512-byte units per POSIX. The task note might be referring to something else.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the task note: \"Blocks from st_blocks*512\". Maybe this means the FileInfo.Blocks stores BYTE count (not block count)? No, the PRD explicitly says `Blocks int64 // number of 512-byte blocks allocated`. So I'll use `st.Blocks` directly.\n\nNow I need to write the implementation files, add the golang.org/x/sys dependency, and then build and test. For the modification time, both Darwin and Linux have Timespec structs with Sec and Nsec fields, so I can use `time.Unix(st.Mtim.Sec, st.Mtim.Nsec)` to construct the timestamp.\n\nI'm deciding whether to use the lower-level `syscall.Stat` and `syscall.Lstat` functions directly in sys.go, or if I should use the higher-level `os.Stat` and `os.Lstat` instead.\n\nActually, the better approach is to call `os.Stat` and then extract the underlying `syscall.Stat_t` through the `Sys()` method on the returned `FileInfo`. This avoids making redundant syscalls and is more portable across platforms.\n\nImplementing the Stat function... I'm verifying that `unix.IoctlGetWinsize` works consistently across both Darwin and Linux by checking the available syscall functions and their signatures.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the implementation with proper error wrapping following the coding standards, wrapping errors with context using `fmt.Errorf`.\n\nWriting the terminal functions... Now I'm thinking through the imports I need across these files. The main sys.go file will need fmt, os, signal handling, sync, syscall, time, and the unix package from golang.org/x/sys. The platform-specific files for Darwin and Linux just need syscall and time since they're handling the lower-level system calls. I need to make sure syscall is imported in sys.go since I'm using syscall.Stat_t as a type in the fillFromStat function. I need to verify that `syscall.SIGWINCH` and `syscall.SIGPIPE` are available on both Darwin and Linux, and since `syscall.Signal` implements `os.Signal`, I can pass them directly to `signal.Notify`. Now I'm ready to write out the complete implementation.\n\nFor the SIGWINCH handler, `sync.Once` guarantees the resize logic runs exactly once, which is what I want. The goroutine reading from the signal channel will block forever, which is fine for a daemon. There's a potential issue in tests where multiple calls to OnTerminalResize could accumulate callbacks, but since I'm not testing SIGWINCH handling directly, this shouldn't be a problem.\n\nNow I'll implement all four files.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8789,"cache_read_input_tokens":63677,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8789},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a95a5d2-6a7e-4f46-be9a-8dc65ffccd7e","uuid":"6a608c59-4ccc-44e4-b269-1fc82a28ce9b"}
